# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18947> ,
              <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73533> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q43610> ,
              <https://runeberg.org/eddan/> ,
              <https://www.ne.se/uppslagsverk/encyklopedi/l%C3%A5ng/oden> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nordisk_mytologi> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (a script that matched every quotation word for word against the saved source texts, live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "1. Texts. The two Eddas were downloaded as plain text from Project Gutenberg (queries 1–2): the Prose Edda in Rasmus B. Anderson's translation (1879, eBook #18947), which covers Gylfaginning in full and the main passages of Skáldskaparmál, and the Poetic Edda in Henry Adams Bellows's translation with his notes (1923, eBook #73533). Both translators died more than 70 years ago (Wikidata, query 3). Anderson numbers the paragraphs of Gylfaginning (§1–§59); the locators use those numbers. Bellows's locators name the poem and the stanza, or the note to a stanza."@en ,
                 "2. Selection. The cards cover what the editor's brief asked for, as far as both Eddas attest it: the chief gods and what Gylfaginning says each rules or is known for (§20–§33), the family ties stated in the Eddas, Odin's and Thor's best-known possessions, the beings and worlds of the creation story, Yggdrasil, Bifröst, Valhalla and the main events of Ragnarök (§55–§56, Voluspo 44–56). Each candidate fact was kept only if Anderson's Prose Edda states it and, wherever the poems cover it, Bellows's Poetic Edda agrees; see Selection for what was left out."@en ,
                 "3. Wikidata. The items were found by their English Wikipedia titles (API call 4, wbgetentities with sites=enwiki), which returned the English, Swedish and multilingual labels and aliases, the Swedish Wikipedia sitelinks and every statement of the items; the labels of the statements' values were fetched in a second call (API call 5). Two exploratory searches by name (wbsearchentities, query 6) came first. The English names on the cards are the Wikidata English label, or an English alias where the label differs from the form the Eddas' translators and the English Wikipedia use (Baldr rather than the label Baldur; Idun, Vidar, Huginn and Muninn); the Swedish names are the Wikidata Swedish label or alias, except where step 4 says otherwise."@en ,
                 "4. Swedish. Every Swedish name was compared with the headword of Nationalencyklopedin's article (query 7: the free opening of each article, saved with its URL), with the title of the Swedish Wikipedia article (query 8: the introductions and revision ids, API) and, for names in the poems, with Erik Brate's Swedish Poetic Edda of 1913 (query 9, Project Runeberg). They agree on Oden, Tor, Balder, Loke, Frej, Freja, Njord, Tyr, Heimdall, Idun, Brage, Frigg, Vidar, Höder, Hel, Fenrisulven, Midgårdsormen, Sleipner, Mjölner, Hugin och Munin, Gungner, Bifrost, Yggdrasil, Valhall, Ragnarök, Asgård, Midgård, Jotunheim, Muspelhem, Nifelhem, Surt, Ymer, Mimer and valkyria. NE has no article on Angerboda or Gleipner (the addresses answered with an error page); for these the Swedish Wikipedia titles and the Wikidata Swedish labels agree, and NE's article on Fenrisulven writes \"jättekvinnan Angerboda\". Thor's mother has no Swedish Wikidata label of her own (the item's Swedish label is \"Fjorgyn\"); the Swedish form Jord is from the Swedish Wikipedia article Tor (\"son till ”allfadern” Oden och asynjan Jord\") and Thor's Swedish Wikidata alias \"Jords son\". Her other names in the card's note are Anderson's English forms Fjorgyn and Hlodyn (Vocabulary and §56); the Swedish forms Fjorgyn and Lodyn are Brate's (Völuspå 56, \"Lodyns\" and \"Fjorgyns son\"; query 12), Fjorgyn also being the Wikidata Swedish label and the Swedish Wikipedia title (which writes the other name Lödyn). The Swedish questions and answers were written by the compiler following NE's and Swedish Wikipedia's usage: asarna in lower case, Snorres Edda for the Prose Edda, Ragnarök with a capital as in NE and Wikidata (Brate writes ragnarök). Names in the notes: Jörmungand from the Swedish Wikipedia article Midgårdsormen (\"Jörmungand (Jǫrmungandr\"; Wikidata's Swedish aliases have only Jörmundgand and similar forms); Svadilfare from the Wikidata Swedish label of Q1125073 and the Swedish Wikipedia article Svadilfare; Ivaldes söner from Anderson's \"the sons of Ivalde\" (§44) with the Swedish genitive, as NE also writes it (Wikidata's Q37499380 has the Swedish label Ivaldi). The English form Ivaldi in the same note is Bellows's (Grimnismol 43, \"Ivaldi’s sons\", and his note to it)."@en ,
                 "5. Cross-check. Every card was checked against at least two sources: the Prose Edda (Anderson) and, where the poems cover the fact, the Poetic Edda (Bellows), plus Wikidata (names and, where it has them, the statement) and Nationalencyklopedin, or the Swedish Wikipedia where NE has no article or does not cover the fact. The evidence of each card quotes what each source says. The script make_dossier.py (query 10) wrote the dossier from the card data: it checks that every quoted passage occurs word for word in the saved copy of its source (the Gutenberg and Runeberg texts, the NE openings and the Wikipedia extracts; in the NE openings, the spaces that removing the HTML tags left before punctuation and inside brackets are removed before comparing and quoting), prints where each Anderson and Bellows quotation stands (chapter, poem, stanza or note) so that the locators could be compared by hand, and writes the Wikidata evidence of each card from its checks and the saved entities, failing if the saved data do not satisfy a check. The scripts themselves are not published; the rules above are what they do."@en ,
                 "6. Cards. Fronts are questions in English and Swedish; backs are a name or a short phrase in our own words, with a short note where it helps (for example the meaning of the ravens' names, or how a death at Ragnarök comes about). The answers about what a god rules follow the Prose Edda's own words, shortened: Njord \"rules the course of the wind and checks the fury of the sea\" → the wind and the sea (the card's note adds that he also checks the fury of fire, so a learner who knows the whole sentence is not misled); Freyr \"rules over rain and sunshine, and over the fruits of the earth\". English names follow one convention: the familiar anglicised forms of the English translations and of Wikidata's English labels and aliases, written without the Old Norse letter ð and without acute accents (Njord, Tyr, Idun, Vidar, Jord, Angrboda, Hodr, Mimir, Svadilfari), keeping ö only in the four names English usually writes with it (Mjölnir, Jörmungandr, Bifröst, Ragnarök) and the nominative -r where English keeps it (Freyr, Surtr, Huginn). Where an English and a Swedish name differ only in convention, each language has its own form (Baldr/Balder, Fenrir/Fenrisulven, Jörmungandr/Midgårdsormen). Study direction front to back only: several answers recur (Loki answers three cards, Fenrir and Jörmungandr two each), and many fronts are descriptions that would not make sense as answers."@en ,
                 "7. Machine checks. For every card the builder checked against live Wikidata that the English and Swedish names on the card are labels or aliases of the item (except where noted in quality control, such as Hodr) and, where Wikidata has the statement, the fact itself: Thor is a thunder deity (P31 Q842697), Freyja a love deity (P31 Q1670614), Tyr's domain is war, Bragi's poetry, Freyr's weather and Loki's deception (P2925); Thor's mother is Jord, Baldr's parents Odin and Frigg, Njord's children Freyr and Freyja, Angrboda's and Loki's children Fenrir, Jörmungandr and Hel, Sleipnir's mother Loki (P22, P25, P26, P40); Mjölnir is Thor's and Sleipnir and Gungnir Odin's (P127); Odin is killed by Fenrir, Fenrir by Vidar, Thor and Jörmungandr by each other, Baldr by Hodr, Freyr by Surtr and Ymir by Odin (P157); Bifröst is made of the rainbow (P186). Facts Wikidata does not record (Odin's eye, Tyr's hand, Heimdall and Loki killing each other, Idun's apples, the worlds' descriptions) rest on the Eddas and the cross-check in step 5. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Selection: 44 cards: 11 on the chief gods and what the Prose Edda says they rule or are known for (Odin, Thor, Baldr, Njord, Freyr, Freyja, Tyr, Bragi, Heimdall, Idun, Loki); 8 on family ties stated in the Eddas (Thor's mother Jord, Baldr's parents Odin and Frigg, Njord as father of Freyr and Freyja, Loki's children with Angrboda — Fenrir, Jörmungandr and Hel — and Loki as the mother of Sleipnir); 8 on beings and things (Mjölnir, Sleipnir, Huginn and Muninn, Gungnir, Gleipnir, the valkyries, Ymir, Surtr); 9 on the world (Yggdrasil, Bifröst, Valhalla, Asgard, Midgard, Jotunheim, Muspelheim, Niflheim, Ragnarök); and 8 on stories (Odin's eye, Tyr's hand, the death of Baldr, and who kills whom at Ragnarök). Included only what the Eddas themselves state, with nothing from later retellings, folklore or modern popular culture. Left out: the list of the nine worlds, which neither Edda gives (Bellows's note to Voluspo 2 lists eight and calls the ninth \"uncertain\"), and with it Vanaheim, Alfheim, Svartalfheim and Nidavellir; minor gods and goddesses about whom the Eddas say little (Ull, Forseti, Vali, Hermod, Sif, Nanna, Sigyn, Skadi and the goddesses of Gylfaginning §36) and Frigg, apart from her place as Baldr's mother; claims on which the sources differ, such as Tyr's father (Odin in Snorri, the giant Hymir in Hymiskvitha), Heimdall's mothers or the fate of Mimir's head; and interpretations that are not in the Eddas (Odin as god of war or of the dead, Freyr and Freyja as fertility gods, Loki as a fire god), although the notes keep the Eddas' own statements, such as Freyja's half of the slain. Thor's link to thunder is given by his name and by both translators (Bellows: \"the thunder-god\"; Anderson's vocabulary: \"The god of thunder\"), not spelled out in Snorri's description of him, which calls him the strongest of gods and men; the card keeps the universally used answer \"thunder\" and gives Snorri's description in the note."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from three sources whose terms allow anything: the Prose Edda in Rasmus B. Anderson's translation (1879) and the Poetic Edda in Henry Adams Bellows's translation with his notes (1923), both public domain in the US (Project Gutenberg: \"Public domain in the USA\") and, since both translators died more than 70 years ago (1936 and 1939), also in countries with a term of life plus 70 years; and Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0, for the English and Swedish names and the statements. The selection of cards is our own (the gods, beings, places and events named in the editor's brief and attested in both Eddas), not taken from any list, and the questions, answers and notes are our own wording of facts; the quotations from the translations are in the evidence, as is allowed for public-domain texts in any case. Erik Brate's Swedish Poetic Edda (1913; Brate died in 1924) is also in the public domain and was used to verify the Swedish name forms; it also supplied the Swedish form Lodyn in one note. Nationalencyklopedin (all rights reserved; its texts may be quoted only non-commercially) and the Swedish Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) were used for verification only: they confirmed the Swedish names and facts the content sources gave, and no wording or selection was taken from them, apart from two single Swedish name forms that the Swedish Wikipedia supplied or confirmed (Jord, also written so in Anderson's translation, and Jörmungand), which are facts and not protected wording; the short passages quoted from them in the provenance report are evidence for the checks, not part of the cards. The CC0 dedication covers the cards only. The quotations from Nationalencyklopedin and the Swedish Wikipedia in the provenance report are short verbatim quotations with their source named, used as evidence under the right of quotation (upphovsrättslagen 22 §) and, for Wikipedia, under CC BY-SA 4.0 with the article and revision cited; they are not released under CC0."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every card against Anderson's Prose Edda and, where the poems cover it, Bellows's Poetic Edda; the names and statements against Wikidata; the Swedish names against Nationalencyklopedin, the Swedish Wikipedia and Brate's Swedish Edda; every quotation verified word for word against the saved texts by make_dossier.py."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards, the sources and their licences, and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card is stated in the Prose Edda, and every card the poems cover agrees with the Poetic Edda; Wikidata agrees with the Eddas wherever it has the statement, and NE or the Swedish Wikipedia confirms every Swedish name. No Wikidata check was skipped because Wikidata disagreed with the Eddas; the findings below are differences in naming, gaps in Wikidata or the reference works, and facts left out because the sources differ."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards against Brodeur's independent translation of the Prose Edda, live Wikidata and the Swedish Wikipedia; counts and description."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors found. One ambiguous front (freyja-domain) was tied to the Prose Edda's own words; the description now names the Swedish Wikipedia beside NE; the mistletoe checks are explained in the evidence."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards, title, description and keywords: English and Swedish wording, name forms against Wikidata, NE, the Swedish Wikipedia and Brate, and language tags."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Language tags and Swedish names were correct. The English names now follow one stated convention, the calqued Swedish front of ragnarok-thor and four awkward fronts were reworded, Sleipnir is åttabent as in NE, Jord has its own Swedish Wikipedia evidence and the English notes use typographic quotes."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every source's licence page, every quotation against re-downloaded sources, the method, queries, licensing text and the generated report."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Licensing was sound. The gungnir evidence now quotes the Anderson sentences that name Ivaldi's sons, the provenance of the Swedish note names is documented, the paragraph range is corrected, the NE wording is gone from the Hugin and Munin note, the licensing text says the report's quotations are not CC0, the quotation check is named in the first method paragraph and the helper scripts' URL templates are recorded. The double numbering of the rendered method was left as in the other decks."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 14 cards changed in rounds 1–3 and every third card (27 distinct cards) against re-downloaded Gutenberg texts, live NE and Swedish Wikipedia pages and live Wikidata (all 124 checks); metadata, the quality-control log and a validation run."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; three suggestions, all applied. The gungnir evidence now cites Bellows for the English form Ivaldi, the njord-domain note says the Prose Edda also has him check the fury of fire, and the licensing text says which single Swedish name forms came from the Swedish Wikipedia."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 44 cards against the dossier's evidence, with every Anderson and Bellows quotation matched word for word against re-downloaded Gutenberg texts; Swedish names, style and language tags; selection counts, description, keywords and study direction; the fixes of round 4."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Round 4's fixes were confirmed in place. One warning (thor-mother: the sources give other names for Jord) and two wording suggestions (tyr-hand, gleipnir); all three were verified and applied."@en .

<https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18947>
    dcterms:title "The Younger Edda (the Prose Edda), translated by Rasmus B. Anderson (1879; Project Gutenberg eBook #18947)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Snorri Sturluson; translated by Rasmus Bjørn Anderson (1846–1936)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Project Gutenberg's record of eBook #18947 (https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18947): \"Copyright Public domain in the USA.\" The translation was first published in 1879 (title page: \"Copyright, 1879, By S. C. Griggs and Company.\"), and the translator Rasmus Bjørn Anderson died in 1936 (Wikidata Q3436547, \"American diplomat, translator, and scholar (1846–1936)\"), more than 70 years ago, so it is also out of copyright in countries with a term of the author's life plus 70 years. Snorri's text itself is from the 13th century. Project Gutenberg's licence (https://www.gutenberg.org/policy/license.html): \"If you strip the Project Gutenberg license and all references to Project Gutenberg from the text, you are left with a text unrestricted by U.S. intellectual property law.\""@en .

<https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73533>
    dcterms:title "The Poetic Edda, translated with notes by Henry Adams Bellows (1923; Project Gutenberg eBook #73533)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Translated by Henry Adams Bellows (1885–1939)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Project Gutenberg's record of eBook #73533 (https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73533): \"Copyright Public domain in the USA.\" The ebook's header: \"Original publication: New York: The American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923\". The translator Henry Adams Bellows died on 29 December 1939 (Wikidata Q11336703), more than 70 years ago, so the translation and its notes are also out of copyright in countries with a term of life plus 70 years. The poems themselves are medieval."@en .

